From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 11:56:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] powrprof: the platform's role, the one power scheme, and
 power notifications.

PowerDeterminePlatformRoleEx answered desktop with a FIXME.  Windows
reads the power profile the maker put in the ACPI tables; the part of
that there is here is the SMBIOS chassis type, so a portable, laptop,
notebook, hand held, sub notebook or convertible enclosure is a mobile
platform, a tablet or detachable a slate to the second version and
mobile to the first, a server chassis, rack mount or blade an
enterprise server, an embedded PC an appliance, and anything else a
desktop.  A version past the second answers as the second.

PowerGetActiveScheme answered ERROR_CALL_NOT_IMPLEMENTED.  There is one
scheme, balanced, as on a computer with modern standby, which is all
the Windows machine measured enumerates: PowerGetActiveScheme gives it
in memory LocalFree frees, PowerEnumerate of schemes gives it and then
ERROR_NO_MORE_ITEMS, asking for the size with ERROR_MORE_DATA, and
PowerReadFriendlyName names it from a string resource and finds no
other.

PowerSettingRegisterNotification and PowerRegisterSuspendResumeNotification
take only DEVICE_NOTIFY_CALLBACK, and anything else leaves the handle
alone with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER; the callbacks are user32's to call,
so they are registered there.  PowerRegisterForEffectivePowerModeNotifications,
for versions up to the second, calls its callback from another thread
soon after with the mode, balanced, and
PowerUnregisterFromEffectivePowerModeNotifications, which was missing,
takes the registration back and waits for a call under way.

